# APPLY-ONE, the DESCRIPTOR subject — c0 §43 `law.brace` row 1, §44
# `apply.edge` / `law.pack.shape`. gap[092], the resolving half.
#
# THE LAW. `apply(descriptor)(fieldpack) -> a value the descriptor describes`.
# The subject decides what applying means; the FACE does not, and the parser
# resolves nothing. Before this, the call path won unconditionally and
# `point{ x = 3.0, y = 4.0 }` reported `call to undeclared function 'point'`.
#
# WHY A SECOND FILE and not more rows in `apply_one_pack_proof.id`. That file
# carries `kind = @{ eof = 0, ident = 1 }`, and a top-level function with a
# non-`any` return type in the same file makes its `print(kind.ident)` emit
# `lua_to_display_str(1)` — an `int` where a `lua_Value` is wanted. MEASURED on
# the pre-GAP-092 compiler (`HEAD~1`, built in isolation), so it is a
# pre-existing defect this work neither caused nor repairs. The rows below need
# a descriptor-typed callee, so they live here. Recorded rather than worked
# around silently: a fixture that quietly avoids a defect hides it.
#
# Every row asserts a VALUE, except the last, which asserts a REPRESENTATION
# and therefore cannot be asserted here at all — see ROW 4.

point: { x: f64, y: f64 }

# A parameter typed by the DESCRIPTOR. This is what makes ROW 2 a composition
# proof rather than a second binding proof.
sum: f64 = (p: point)
    p.x + p.y

main = ()
    # ROW 1 — the descriptor subject through the brace face, by value. Read
    # against `apply_one_pack_proof.id` ROW 1, which constructs the SAME value
    # through the two forms that always worked (a typed binding plus a bare
    # pack). Same digits from two edges, or one of them stopped describing
    # `point`.
    q = point{ x = 3.0, y = 4.0 }
    print(q.x)
    print(q.y)

    # ROW 2 — descriptor application COMPOSES. It is an expression, not a
    # special right-hand side, so it goes wherever a value goes — here straight
    # into a parameter typed by the descriptor it constructs. A construction
    # that only works after `=` is a statement wearing an expression's clothes.
    print(sum(point{ x = 10.0, y = 11.0 }))

    # ROW 3 — POSITIONS as well as labels. §44 `apply.carries` names both, and
    # both are the pack's OWN fields in declaration order. Nothing parallel
    # holds them, which is why this needed no machinery of its own: a second
    # table of labels beside the pack is where the two shapes drift apart.
    print(sum(point{ 1.0, 20.0 }))

    # ROW 4 — `law.pack.shape`: "a braced argument NEED NOT MATERIALIZE A
    # TABLE." This row cannot be proven by a printed digit and is not pretended
    # to be — a heap table prints `21` just as happily as two registers do. The
    # assertion is made by `zig build apply-one`, which reads the emitted C and
    # requires BOTH the compound literal AND zero `lua_table_new_with_capacity`
    # in the constructing function. Measured 2026-08-09:
    #
    #     duo_rec_266508db7bffdac0 q = (duo_rec_266508db7bffdac0){
    #         .x = 3e0,
    #         .y = 4e0
    #     };
    #
    # The digit below is the positive control for that gate row: it proves the
    # fields survived whatever representation was chosen, so a green
    # no-allocation row cannot be green by having emitted nothing.
    print(sum(point{ x = 0.5, y = 0.25 }))

